A store is four methods over raw documents, by collection name and slug. `createContent` does everything else: validation, unique slugs, renames, references. So a store stays small.

```ts
interface ContentStore {
  list(collection: string): Promise<unknown[]>;
  get(collection: string, slug: string): Promise<unknown>;
  put(collection: string, doc: Doc): Promise<void>;
  remove(collection: string, slug: string): Promise<void>;
}
```

- `list` returns every stored document of the collection, each with `slug` set, or `[]`.
- `get` returns the document under `slug`, or `undefined`. If it exists but can't be read, throw.
- `put` stores the document under `doc.slug`, replacing what's there.
- `remove` deletes the document under `slug`, and does nothing if there's none.

Return raw data: `createContent` normalizes and validates it.

## Example: session storage

This site lets anyone edit it without a server. Its admin saves through `createContent` in the browser, over a store kept in the tab's session storage. Here's that store, slightly shortened.

It stores only **changes** on top of the published content, under one key: a saved document, or `null` for a deleted one. Clearing that key returns the reader to the published site, which is what the **Reset** button does.

```ts
import { isValidSlug, type ContentStore, type Doc } from '@mortar-kit/core';

type Changes = Record<string, Record<string, Doc | null>>;

export function createSessionStorageStore(options: {
  baseline: Record<string, Doc[]>;
  key?: string;
}): ContentStore & { hasChanges(): boolean; clear(): void } {
  const key = options.key ?? 'mortar:changes';
  const baseline = new Map(
    Object.entries(options.baseline).map(([c, docs]) => [c, new Map(docs.map((d) => [d.slug, d]))]),
  );
  const read = (): Changes => JSON.parse(sessionStorage.getItem(key) ?? '{}');
  const change = (collection: string, slug: string, doc: Doc | null) => {
    const changes = read();
    (changes[collection] ??= {})[slug] = doc;
    sessionStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(changes));
  };

  return {
    async list(collection) {
      const merged = new Map(baseline.get(collection));
      for (const [slug, doc] of Object.entries(read()[collection] ?? {})) {
        if (doc === null) merged.delete(slug);
        else merged.set(slug, doc);
      }
      return [...merged].map(([slug, doc]) => ({ ...doc, slug }));
    },
    async get(collection, slug) {
      const changes = read()[collection] ?? {};
      const doc = slug in changes ? changes[slug] : baseline.get(collection)?.get(slug);
      return doc ? { ...doc, slug } : undefined;
    },
    async put(collection, doc) {
      if (!isValidSlug(doc.slug)) throw new Error(`Invalid slug "${doc.slug}"`);
      change(collection, doc.slug, doc);
    },
    async remove(collection, slug) {
      change(collection, slug, null);
    },
    hasChanges: () => Object.values(read()).some((folder) => Object.keys(folder).length > 0),
    clear: () => sessionStorage.removeItem(key),
  };
}
```

The full version is in `docs/src/content/session-store.ts`. It also copies documents in and out, survives storage that's missing or unreadable, and drops the change when you delete a document you created in the same tab.

Session storage belongs to one tab. Edits last while the tab is open and survive reloads. Same-origin iframes in the tab share it, which is how the admin's preview sees your saves. No one else ever sees them.

## Using it

```ts
const store = createSessionStorageStore({ baseline });
const content = createContent({ store, collections, bricks });

// The admin saves into this tab:
<Admin content={content} ... />

// Reset to the published site:
store.clear();
```

On this site, the published documents (`baseline`) are JSON files bundled into the app, and the server renders them. Once a page has loaded in the browser, it reads again through the session storage store, so your edits replace the published content. See [About this site](/docs/about-this-site).
